 { A protected passed pawn is a great asset in any endgame. The next one is a simple example. }  
 *

[Event "Mastering Endgame Strategy"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/12520529/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "12.2"]
[White "2.4 Protected Passed Pawns"]
[Black "Example 80 by Alburt- L."]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "8/8/2p2k2/pp3Pp1/6P1/PKP5/8/8 w - - 0 1"]

 { Thanks to his protected passed pawn on f5, and its restricting effect on the enemy king, White is much better. With his next move he initiates an attack on the black queenside pawns. } 1. { -KEY- } a4 $1 ( { Also possible is } 1. c4 $5 { in the same spirit; e.g. } Ke5 2. Kc3 Kd6 ( { or } 2. a4 Kf6 3. cxb5 cxb5 4. Kd4 { and the king soon liquidates the enemy pawns } ) 
3. cxb5 cxb5 4. Kd4 Kc6 5. Ke5 $1 b4 ( { equally hopeless is } 5. a4 a4 6. Kd4 Kd6 7. Kc3 Kc5 8. f6 Kd6 9. Kb4 { etc } ) 
6. axb4 axb4 7. Kd4 $1 { and wins. } ) 
Ke5 2. axb5 ( { But not } 2. c4 $2 b4 { and Black has created his own protected passed pawn with a draw - Alburt. } ) 
cxb5 3. c4 a4+ ( { Now if } 3... b4 { then } 4. c5 { with a decisive square of separated passed pawns. } ) 
4. Kc3 ( { In order to eliminate the most dangerous black pawn, the one on the c-file. In contrast, after } 4. Kb4 $2 a3 $1 5. Kxa3 bxc4 6. Kb4 Kd4 $1 7. f6 c3 { Black queens as well. } ) 
bxc4 5. Kxc4 { Next White eliminates the a-pawn and brings his king to the opposite flank with a trivial win. } 
 *

[Event "Mastering Endgame Strategy"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/12520530/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "12.3"]
[White "2.4 Protected Passed Pawns"]
[Black "Example 81- Tal- M. vs. Fuchs- R.- Kislovodsk 1964"]
[Result "1-0"]
[FEN "8/4k3/1nB1pp1p/p3P1p1/3P1PPP/P1K5/8/8 w - - 0 1"]

 ( { Black has just played ...g6-g5 to avoid 1.g5 fixing a weakness on g6 . Tal found a simple way of realizing his material advantage. } ) 
1. { -KEY- } exf6+ $1 { This and the next two moves produce a strong protected passed pawn on the h-file. } Kxf6 2. fxg5+ hxg5 3. h5 $1 { From now on this pawn will severely limit Black's king. } Kg7 4. d5 $1 { The most attractive follow-up, which leaves the knight helpless against the invasion of the white king. } exd5 5. Kd4 Nc4 6. Bb5 $1 ( { More technical than } 6. a4 Kf6 { followed by ...Ne5 - Tal. } ) 
Nd6 ( { Or } 6... Nxa3 7. Bd3 $1 { applying the magic distance, with an easy win after } Nc4 8. Bxc4 dxc4 9. Kxc4 { etc. } ) 
7. Bd3 Ne8 8. Bf5 Nf6 9. Ke5 ( { In view of } 9. Ke5 Kf7 10. h6 { Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

