[White "2.4 Protected Passed Pawns"]
[Black "Example 83- Khalifman- A. vs. Shaposhnikov- E.- St Petersburg 1998"]
[Result "0-1"]
[FEN "8/r4pp1/1k5p/1p1p3P/1P1P2P1/1BK2P2/8/8 b - - 0 1"]

 { Here White is trying to survive by means of a fortress, but Black's clever maneuver puts an end to any such illusions. } 1... { -KEY- } Rc7+ 2. Kb2 Rc4 $1 { Forcing a pawn ending where the protected passed pawn secures an easy win. } 3. Bxc4 bxc4 4. f4 ( { Or } 4. Ka2 Kb5 5. Ka3 c3 $1 { with similar play to the game. } ) 
Kb5 ( 4... f6 { at once also works, as observed by Benner } ) 
5. Ka3 ( { Or } 5. Kc3 Ka4 { and White soon runs out of moves - Hecht. } ) 
f6 6. f5 c3 $1 { By trading the b- and c-pawns, Black makes the d4-pawn an easy target. } 7. Kb3 c2 ( { In view of } 7... c2 8. Kxc2 Kxb4 9. Kd3 Kb3 { etc, White resigned. } ) 

 0-1

