[Black "Example 84- Dearing- E. vs. Erenburg- S.- Budapest 2004"]
[Result "1-0"]
[FEN "2k5/pbn2p2/2p1p3/4P1p1/Nq2P3/1P1Q4/4BPP1/6K1 w - - 0 1"]

 { The idea of creating a protected passed pawn can take place in earlier stages of the game as well. Here is one example.At this point Black was probably hoping to activate his position with ... c6-c5, but after the next, strong opposing move he never gets time for that. } 1. { -KEY- } Qd6 $1 Qxd6 ( { a) After } 1... a5 2. Nc5 ) 
 ( { b) or } 1... Qe1 2. Bf1 Kb8 ( { if } 2... Ba6 { then } 3. Qf8 Kb7 4. Nc5 Kb6 5. Nd7 Ka5 6. Qa3 { - Erenburg } ) 
3. Nc5 { Black is also suffering. } ) 
2. exd6 Nb5 3. e5 { The protected passed pawn is a fact, while Black has a really bad bishop on b7. } Nd4 { Unfortunately for Black, the desirable } ( 3... f6 { fails tactically to } 4. Bxb5 cxb5 5. exf6 ) 
4. Bc4 c5 ( { In the event of passive defence with } 4... Kd8 5. Nc5 Bc8 { one attractive plan is } 6. Kh2 { followed by Kh3-g4. } ) 
5. Nxc5 Ba8 ( 5... Bd5 { was slightly more promising, although after } 6. Bxd5 exd5 7. Kh2 Nc6 8. Nd3 Kd7 9. Kg3 Ke6 ( { both here and later, } 9... f6 { fails to } 10. Nc5 ) 
10. f4 $1 { White is winning - Erenburg. } ) 
6. f3 Nc6 7. Nd3 Na5 8. Kf2 { With a healthy extra pawn and more active pieces, the rest is easy for White. } Kd7 9. Bb5+ Bc6 10. Nc5+ Ke8 11. b4 $1 Bxb5 12. bxa5 Bc6 13. Kg3 Bb5 14. Kg4 Bf1 15. g3 Be2 16. Ne4 Kd7 17. Nxg5 f6 18. exf6 Kxd6 19. Nh7 $1 
 1-0

